### interconversion-and-timescale
- Tautomerism is one instance of a general fact that the carrier has to hold: whether two forms are one
  substance depends on how fast they interconvert relative to the observation, and the field's deepest
  case is thalidomide. The molecule has one stereocentre, at C-3 of the glutarimide ring, and the
  configuration is labile: enantiomerically pure thalidomide racemizes in aqueous buffer at pH 7.4 and
  37 °C with a half-life of 266 ± 10 minutes for (S) and 295 ± 24 for (R), and in human serum with a
  half-life of 21.8 ± 1.0 minutes for (S) and 32.9 for (R). The enantiomers were resolved in 1970, nine
  years after the withdrawal.
- The textbook account — (S) is the teratogen, (R) is the sedative, so a pure enantiomer would have been
  safe — does not survive those numbers, and the literature says so: the interconversion in vivo raises
  what the field calls the thalidomide paradox, that teratogenicity is not observed in animal experiments
  given (R) despite ready racemization, and the 1967 rabbit work found both enantiomers teratogenic. A
  2018 hypothesis explains the paradox by self-disproportionation — a fraction epimerises and precipitates
  as the (R/S) heterodimer while enantiomerically pure material stays in solution. Roughly 10,000 children
  were affected, with many more pregnancies lost.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is written in `chem/identity.cub` and it computes. `Interconv` carries the
  interconversions of a set of forms with their half-lives; `ic-within` says which an observation may
  identify — those that interconvert faster than the window the observation takes — so the identification
  is data rather than a convention. With thalidomide's serum half-lives as that data, `td-one-in-a-day`
  computes that over a day's exposure the two forms are one, and `td-two-in-ten-minutes` computes that
  over ten minutes they are two. Same molecule, same measurements, opposite verdicts, and both correct.
  So "are these the same substance" has no answer apart from the observation; a carrier that answers it
  once, for every purpose — which is what a canonical string does, and what InChI's tautomer rules do by
  fiat — answers it wrongly for most purposes. And the clinical form of that error is the belief that
  resolving the enantiomers would have prevented the harm: the body walks the path whatever the bottle
  contains.
**links:** tautomers · chirality · automorphism-is-physical · regulatory-identity · the-construction ·
kinetic-stability · metamorphic-folds.

### organometallics
- The graph model has a boundary and the field knows where it is. SMILES admits four bond types — "Single,
  double, triple, and aromatic bonds are represented by the symbols –, =, #, and :" — and molecular
  inorganic chemistry exceeds that: d-orbital overlap allows bond orders higher than three, metal centers
  occur in many coordination environments, and (organo)metal compounds are often characterized by extensive
  electron delocalization and multi-centric bonding "which cannot be captured in simple Lewis formulas".
  Ferrocene, [Fe(η⁵–C₅H₅)₂], already defeats the cyclic-structure convention.
- InChI's treatment is to disconnect the metal bonds, and the consequence is stated in the open: "The
  connection of metal bonds is by definition disconnected which makes most inorganic InChIs meaningless at
  the moment." IUPAC Project 2009-040-2-800 on representation of organometallic and coordination compounds
  commenced in November 2010 and the working group is "working slowly towards a proposal"; the project's own
  risk assessment reads "Organometallic InChI fundamental to integrity and perception of InChI: reputational
  risk if left unaddressed – possible barrier to adoption". A 2023 workshop at RWTH Aachen took up inorganic
  stereochemistry, which the current version does not touch at all, and Yerin's stated requirements include
  procedures to determine configurationally inequivalent ligands and a bond model for delocalized ligands.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, now a theorem rather than a limit:* a graph with four bond types is a model
  of localized two-centre bonding, and where the bonding is delocalized and multi-centric the graph is not
  the object — so identity there is not graph identity, and keeping the automorphisms of a wrong carrier
  fixes nothing. The right carrier is written and the statement is sharp. `HMol` in `chem/identity.cub` is
  bonding as it is: the atoms with their elements, a type of bonds, the atoms each bond is incident on, and
  the electron count of each bond. `TwoCentred` states the property the standard model enforces, without
  counting anything: any three atoms a bond is incident on include two that are the same atom.
  `hmol-of-moleq` embeds the standard representation into the general one — the bonds are the pairs of
  atoms that have one, each incident on exactly those two, the electron count read off the order — so
  nothing of the localized model is lost by working in the general carrier. `hmol-two-centred` proves that
  every molecule the embedding can produce has the two-centred property. And `bridge` is one B–H–B bridge
  of diborane written as what it is, a single bond of two electrons incident on three atoms, with
  `bridge-not-two-centred` proving it is not the embedding of any molecule of the standard
  representation. So the boundary the field has been working at since 2009 is not a difficulty with an
  algorithm: the carrier provably cannot hold the object, and a carrier that can holds it with nothing
  added. "Configurationally inequivalent ligands" is then an orbit computation under the automorphisms of
  this object, and those are relabellings of atoms and of bonds together preserving incidence.
