### the-digest-has-an-error-bar
- The InChIKey is the hashed form used as a database key: 27 characters — a 14-character first block for the
  molecular skeleton, an 8-character second block for stereo, isotopic and (in non-standard keys) tautomeric
  isomerism, three flag characters and two dashes — computed as a truncated SHA-256 of the InChI string.
- Its designers publish its failure rate. Pletnev, Erin, McNaught, Blinov, Tchekhovskoi and Heller,
  "InChIKey collision resistance: an experimental testing" (*J. Cheminform.* 4:39, 2012), state the
  position: "due to the very essence of hash functions, collisions (the same InChIKey for different
  InChIs/structures) are unavoidable in very large collections", and "The appearance of collision itself does
  not compromise the signature as collision-free hashing is impossible; the only viable approach is to set
  and keep a reasonable level of collision resistance which is sufficient for typical applications." The
  theoretical estimate is about 2.2 × 10¹⁵ distinguishable keys, giving a 1.3% probability of a single
  first-block collision in a database of one billion compounds and 0.014% at one hundred million; the design
  goal was to avoid collisions up to several thousand isomers per skeleton. They tested the short second
  block exhaustively against all 67,108,864 stereoisomers of spongistatin I. The first InChIKey collision
  was reported in 2009.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is the cleanest possible confirmation of the first clause of (10.22). `digest(d₁) =
  digest(d₂)` does not imply that `d₁` and `d₂` are the same stored representation, and the field has
  published the probability that it fails to. The authors' position is correct on its own terms — a hash is
  a hash and they chose a defensible resistance level — and it is precisely why a hash match is evidence
  about a representation rather than an identity: the store can use it as a fast index and has to keep the
  relation it actually stands for alongside. A registration system that treats the key as the identity has
  adopted a 0.014% error rate on the question of whether two substances are the same, at a database size the
  CAS Registry passed in 2015.
**links:** the-three-identities · canonicalization · identity-authority · molecular-graph.

### identity-authority
- The field's working solution to entity resolution is an authority. The CAS Registry began in 1965 as a
  project to uniquely identify and track chemical substances; CAS, a division of the American Chemical
  Society, describes itself as "the world's authority for chemical information" and the Registry as "the
  only complete and accurate source of unique identifiers"; it registered its hundred-millionth substance on
  30 June 2015, about 75 million of those added in the preceding decade, with the records curated by CAS
  scientists from publicly disclosed literature.
- The identifiers are property. CAS Registry Numbers are copyright CAS/ACS, the license forbids compiling
  substantial lists, and when Antony Williams reported that CAS would not permit CAS numbers in Wikipedia
  curated from the official database, Murray-Rust's summary was: "They would make excellent identifiers for
  the semantic web, except that they are closed", with Wikipedia then holding between one and two thousand
  chemical substances, about 0.01% of the Registry. CAS Common Chemistry, opened in 2009 and expanded in
  2021, publishes about 500,000 substances — roughly half a percent of the Registry.
- The open identifier's own history runs the other way: InChI was developed by IUPAC and NIST from 2000 to
  2005 with non-proprietary format and algorithms, was LGPL through version 1.04, moved to a custom
  IUPAC-InChI Trust licence for 1.05 and 1.06, and has been MIT-licensed since 1.07 (1.07.5, February 2026),
  with the source now on GitHub.
- *Claude's gloss:* Murray-Rust's complaint that "there is no authority that catalogues chemistry and
  assigns open identifiers" is exact if the word *open* carries the weight: the authority exists and is
  closed, and the open alternative is an algorithm whose identity relation is the partial one documented
  above. Those are the only two options the field has had, and §10.8 is the third: definitions are
  content-addressed, each identity relation is recorded with its evidence and the strongest available is
  used for the operation, a peer sends a definition with its dependency closure and its proofs, and "No
  global namespace is needed for definition identity". Identity by authority and identity by evidence are
  different institutions, and the first is why a hundred million substances are legible only through a
  subscription. This is the same object as `data-liberation` in the workflow.
**links:** the-digest-has-an-error-bar · the-three-identities · canonicalization · workflow:
data-liberation.

### the-three-identities
- (10.22) applied: two records with equal digests are the same stored representation; two objects
  definitionally equal are the same object in the language; two objects related by `SemEq` are the same
  chemical entity under a stated relation. There is no single `SemEq` for chemistry, and the relation
  differs by case:
  - **tautomers** — a path, with conditions, under which only some properties descend;
  - **stereoisomers** — distinct objects, related by an equivalence that does not commute with the
    orientation component;
  - **salts, hydrates, solvates** — one shared active moiety observed in different solids, so a conclusion
    over the moiety's fiber persists while solubility and dosing conclusions do not;
  - **isotopologues** — the same graph with different vertex masses, physically distinct by the kinetic
    isotope effect: deutetrabenazine was approved in 2017 as its own drug because deuterium substitution
    slows its metabolism, a patentable and clinical distinction invisible to an object keyed on element
    identity alone. InChI has an Isotopologues working group, chaired by Moseley, for this reason;
  - **polymorphs** — identical composition and constitution, different solid, different behavior.
- *Claude's gloss:* the store records each relation with its evidence and uses the strongest available for
  the operation at hand, which is §10.8's rule. A hash match is evidence about a stored representation and
  nothing more, and the practice of treating it as chemical identity is why cross-database merging is manual
  and lossy in exactly these five cases. Murray-Rust's objection is the same one from inside the field: the
  identifier "is now being overused and badly used", and there is no authority that catalogues chemistry and
  assigns open identifiers, because the identity of many compounds is genuinely difficult.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, computed on six documented pairs:* the three relations are now a table
  rather than a distinction. `record-pairs` holds six pairs of records with the three relations as they
  actually stand — α- and β-D-glucopyranose (different InChIs, different constructions, one substance in
  water: the interconversion class at 36:64 that a bottle labelled D-glucose contains); (R,R)- and
  meso-tartaric acid (different on all three); a keto and an enol form under standard InChI, whose
  tautomer invariance is partial (one identifier, different constructions, one substance); ritonavir Form I
  and Form II (the same molecule, so the same InChI and the same molecular construction, and not the same
  substance); an InChIKey collision pair (the same key, two molecules, two substances); and one record
  against itself, so that the counts are over a list holding the trivial case rather than one selected to
  prove a point. Three counts come out: the digest disagrees with sameness of substance on three of the
  six, the molecular construction disagrees with it on three, and the digest and the construction disagree
  with each other on two (`pairs-digest-wrong`, `pairs-defn-wrong`, `pairs-digest-ne-defn`,
  `three-relations-distinct`). So the three relations are pairwise distinct, and the witnesses are not
  hypothetical.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, which way each disagreement runs:* the direction is the part that costs. The
  digest says the same of ritonavir's two forms and of a colliding pair, and different of glucose's two
  anomers. The molecular construction says the same of ritonavir's two forms for the honest reason that it
  is the same molecule — so a record that carries the molecule faithfully still does not carry the
  substance, which is the whole case for `Solid` and `polymorph-not-determined`. The interconverting pair
  wants `Taut` and `Interconv`, with the composition a function of the condition; the colliding pair wants
  a digest that is injective, which `collision-not-injective` shows the key is not.
