## II. Observation

### binding-observation
- An affinity is not an edge weight. §2.2's `Rel(R) ∶= ∑_a ∑_b R(a,b)` (2.10) makes a binding claim an
  inhabitant holding the molecule, the target, the value and what produced it, and over fixed carriers the
  instance space is the family on `A×B` with both projections — "a bare equivalence of total spaces forgets
  which inputs and outputs the witnesses relate."
- An assay is an observation `o ∶ H → O` over experimental histories, and §1's
  `Warrants(y,P) ∶= ∏_{(h,p)∶Fib_o(y)} P(h)` (1.3) states exactly what a reading establishes: a property
  holds for every history compatible with it. The fiber over a potency reading contains compound
  aggregation, fluorescence interference, redox cycling, thiol reactivity and assay-component chelation, so
  "this compound inhibits this target" is usually not what the reading warrants, while "this reading was
  obtained in this assay" is.
- The filters are the field's empirical substitute. Baell and Holloway, "New substructure filters for removal
  of pan assay interference compounds (PAINS) from screening libraries and for their exclusion in bioassays"
  (*J. Med. Chem.* 53(7):2719–2740, 8 April 2010), describe substructural features identifying compounds
  that are frequent hitters across many biochemical high-throughput screens and that are not caught by the
  filters used for reactive compounds; 480 compound classes, identified in AlphaScreen assays, and Baell's
  estimate that about 10% of the compounds in a commercially available screening library might show up as
  false positives. Their stated concern is that "these compounds are increasingly prevalent in the
  literature as potential starting points for further exploration, whereas they may not be". The paper has
  over 3,500 citations, and interference characteristics propagate through medicinal chemistry programs and
  compromise their outcomes.
- *Claude's gloss:* a list of 480 substructures derived from one detection technology is a pattern-match
  standing in for the fiber argument the representation cannot express, which is why its scope is contested:
  the question "how frequently are PAINS actually active" (Jasial, Hu & Bajorath) is the question of whether
  the substructure narrows the fiber, and it has to be asked empirically because the warrant was never
  recorded. Refinement shrinks fibers, so a later selectivity panel narrows the fiber rather than replacing
  the earlier record, and every conclusion already established persists (§1). Models trained on activity
  records take the stronger claim as the label.
