### structure-elucidation
- This is (1.1)–(1.3) as an experimental method. A high-resolution mass measurement observes molecular
  formula, so its fiber is the formulas within the instrument's tolerance, and a formula's own fiber is its
  isomer space. Each further experiment is a refinement `o = h ∘ o′`, and because refinement shrinks fibers
  the formula can be reported before the structure is known. A structure assignment is the claim that the
  fiber has been narrowed to a point.
- The field's error rate is published. Nicolaou and Snyder (*Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.* 44(7):1012–44, 2005)
  reviewed natural-product structural revisions made possible by total synthesis over 1990–2004; Suyama,
  Gerwick and McPhail (*Bioorg. Med. Chem.* 19(22):6675–6701, 2011) tabulated marine revisions for
  2005–2010; a 2022 review in *Natural Product Reports* (Guo and Appendino) summarizes more than 200 cases
  of misassigned marine natural products reported between July 2010 and August 2021. Hexacyclinol is the
  standing case: a 2002 structure, a 2006 synthesis whose spectra did not match, and a revised structure
  confirmed by synthesis and X-ray.
- Their own conclusion is the fiber statement in words: "no single technique can unambiguously assign the
  structure of a complex unknown compound, but the synergistic combination of information from different
  techniques can do it with high reliability".
- *Claude's gloss:* the diagnostic is identical across the revisions — someone exhibited one structure
  compatible with the data instead of showing the fiber was a singleton. Inhabiting one element of a fiber
  and proving a property over the whole fiber are different types, and the field has no notation that
  distinguishes them, which is why the error recurs with better instruments. Automorphism-orbit signal
  counts are one computable constraint on the fiber that the standard object cannot supply.
**links:** automorphism-is-physical · nmr-prediction · chirality · binding-observation · structure-revision · metabolite-annotation.

### metabolite-annotation
- The place where chemistry is most explicit that identity is an inference from an observation is
  untargeted mass spectrometry, and the numbers are stark. "Illuminating the dark matter in metabolomics"
  (*PNAS* 112, 2015) reports that only 1.8% of spectra in an untargeted metabolomics experiment can be
  annotated, with the arithmetic behind it: more than 60 million molecules in PubChem against about
  220,000 reference MS/MS spectra representing roughly 20,000 molecules. A 2025 review of annotation tools
  repeats the same 1.8% as the average still reported. Industry summaries put 10% to 20% of detected
  features at MSI Level 1 or Level 2, leaving 80% to 85% as what the field calls metabolomic dark matter:
  features reliably detected and reproducibly quantified, and chemically anonymous.
- The field responded by typing its own confidence. The MSI levels distinguish an identification confirmed
  against an in-house standard (Level 1) from a putative annotation resting on a high-quality spectral
  library match (Level 2), and the Schymanski scale extends that to five levels from confirmed structure
  down to exact mass alone. And the statistics were missing until recently: before FDR-controlled spectral
  matching, "spectrum–spectrum matches of unknown and library spectra are scored but this score alone
  provides no statement about statistical accuracy of that assignment", leaving untargeted LC-MS/MS with
  "identification results where errors rates are uncontrolled" — a gap closed by empirical-Bayes and
  target-decoy methods over 70 public datasets.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is the construction's `Report` arrived at independently, under pressure, by a
  field that could not avoid it. A level is a warrant type, not a number: `rep-weaken` in
  `chem/identity.cub` carries a report along an implication of warrants — the same molecule and the same
  value resting on less — which is exactly what reporting a Level 1 identification as a Level 2 annotation
  does, and `no-upgrade` is the direction that does not exist, because where no in-house standard exists
  the stronger warrant is uninhabited and no amount of library matching constructs an inhabitant. Two
  consequences the field has not drawn. First, the level belongs inside the record as the warrant itself,
  so that a claim built on a Level 2 annotation carries that fact into everything derived from it
  (`der-retracted`), rather than as a label that is dropped on the first reuse. Second, 1.8% is not a
  coverage problem to be solved by more reference spectra alone: `structure-elucidation`'s fiber is the
  reason, since a spectrum constrains the structure without determining it, and what the remaining 98%
  needs is the constraint recorded as a constraint — a scope over molecules with its warrant, which is
  `Scope` and `Report` together, instead of a name or nothing.
**links:** structure-elucidation · binding-observation · affinity-uncertainty · identity-authority ·
the-construction.

### structure-revision
- Structures are published, accepted, cited, built upon, and then found to be wrong. Nicolaou and Snyder,
  "Chasing molecules that were never there: misassigned natural products and the role of chemical
  synthesis in modern structure elucidation" (*Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.* 44:1012–1044, 2005 — with its own
  corrigendum at 44:2050), set out the case against the common belief that assignment had become routine;
  reviewers of that literature put the count of articles on structural revisions at around a thousand by
  then. Maier's "Structural revisions of natural products by total synthesis" (*Nat. Prod. Rep.*
  26:1105–1124, 2009) covers the following period, a marine-natural-products review covers the cases after
  2005, and a 2023 review asks the question again in its title — are we still chasing molecules that were
  never there — now with quantum-chemical NMR prediction as the instrument of reassignment.
- The mechanism of discovery is the one that matters for the carrier: in many of these cases the error
  surfaced only when the total synthesis of the proposed structure produced a compound whose spectra did
  not match the natural material. That is a fiber argument carried out with glassware — the observation
  did not determine the structure, and the way to find out was to make the candidate and compare.
- *Claude's gloss:* the revision is an edit to the identity of an object that other records point at, and
  nothing in the field's representation carries the edit. The old structure keeps its registry number,
  its database entry and its citations; the new one is a new entry; the papers that computed a property
  from the old structure are not retracted and cannot be enumerated. `Report` and `der-retracted` say
  what the record would have to hold for that enumeration to be possible — the dependency — and
  `retraction-propagation` is where this document states it in the general case. A thousand articles of
  revisions is a thousand instances of a propagation that never happened.
**links:** structure-elucidation · retraction-propagation · molecular-graph · the-construction ·
identity-authority.

### ligand-density
- A crystal structure of a protein with a bound ligand is the primary evidence for a binding mode, and the
  evidence for the ligand itself is its electron density. The real-space correlation coefficient (RSCC)
  measures how well the modelled ligand's calculated density matches the observed density, so it is the
  observation that a pose claim has to be warranted by.
- Weichenberger, Pozharski and Rupp built *Twilight* for this (*Acta Cryst.* F69:195–200, 2013), a
  pre-filtered set of 2,815 protein–ligand complexes deposited with the PDB as of 15 January 2012 whose
  ligand RSCC is below 0.6. Their framing of why: "For consumers of such models it is not trivial to
  determine the degree to which ligand structure modelling is biased by subjective electron density
  interpretation."
- Deller and Rupp, "Models of protein–ligand crystal structures: trust, but verify" (2015), tabulate a
  curated set of 382,588 PDB ligands with density available: 67% have RSCC between 0.9 and 1.0, about 3%
  fall between 0.6 and 0.7 ("very poor fit of ligand to density", roughly 2,100 structures), and about 1%
  below 0.5 ("improbably poor fit"). Their review of the problem classes gives ligands incorrectly
  identified as questionable at 7.4%, incorrectly modelled ligands at 5.2%, ligands with partially missing
  density at 29.2%, glycosylation sites at 31.3%, and ligands placed into density that likely originates
  from mother-liquor components.
- Their epistemology is stated as such: "accepted scientific epistemology requires that strong claims are
  backed by correspondingly strong evidence", and the conclusion is that a small but significant fraction
  of deposited protein–ligand models "are only partially based on evidence and should be used with
  caution".
- *Claude's gloss:* that is `Warrants(y,P)` written by crystallographers. The density is the observation,
  the fiber over it contains the poses compatible with it, and a deposited pose is one element of that fiber
  presented as the answer — the same error as the natural-product assignments, in a different experiment.
  Every docking program trained or validated on deposited poses inherits the unwarranted ones, and the
  partially-missing-density class at 29.2% is not a small tail. A representation that carried the RSCC with
  the pose, rather than beside it in a validation report, would make the strength of the claim part of the
  claim.
**links:** structure-elucidation · binding-observation · space-group-assignment · predictive-validity · docking-observation ·
protein-carrier.

### docking-observation
- A docking score observes one protein conformation and one ligand conformer. The conformers are a family
  over torsion space and the protein has its own ensemble, so the score is an observation twice over, and
  the practical literature reports what that costs.
- Wang, Sun, Yao, Li, Xu, Li, Tian and Hou (*Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys.*, 2016) evaluated ten programs for
  sampling power and scoring power: GOLD reached 59.8% accuracy for the top-scored poses and LeDock 80.8%
  for the best pose among those generated, and they tabulate 72 failure cases that no tested program could
  predict. The distinction between those two numbers is the distinction between generating the right pose
  and scoring it first.
- The structural admission is Jain's: "In nearly all high-throughput docking approaches, protein
  conformation is not among the degrees of freedom being searched", and "acceptable results in pose
  prediction have been generally possible only in the artificial case of re-docking a ligand into" its own
  cognate structure, with non-cognate structures "closer to 20–40% correct". Using conformations from
  multiple complexes raises the average to about 61% across eight targets, and pocket adaptation with
  rescoring to 67% for a single pose family and 75% for the best of two.
- A 2025 cross-docking benchmark finds rigid docking outperforming flexible protocols — "current methods of
  receptor flexibility introduce conformational noise without improving accuracy" — and significant
  cross-docking failure for a deep-learning docking model despite training-test overlap, concluding that
  "current models merely memorize".
- *Claude's gloss:* re-docking into the cognate structure scores the answer against the observation it was
  derived from, which is why it works and why it establishes nothing; the honest number is the non-cognate
  one. The ensemble is the fiber, and a method that does not search protein conformation has fixed a
  coordinate of the fiber by fiat and reports a point. That the deposited poses used as ground truth
  include the unwarranted ones of `ligand-density` compounds the error, and that flexibility "introduces
  noise without improving accuracy" is what widening a fiber without narrowing the evidence does.
**links:** ligand-density · binding-observation · predictive-validity · structure-elucidation ·
structure-predictor-carrier.

### nmr-prediction
- Atoms in one automorphism orbit of the molecular graph are constitutionally equivalent and give one
  signal, so orbit counts predict the number of distinct ¹H and ¹³C resonances. The augmented graph that
  accounts for stereocenters is the one to take orbits of, as in the symmetry-number algorithm.
- *Claude's gloss, corrected 2026-11-18 after checking the literature:* the orbit count against published
  spectra is a demonstration that the construction computes what chemistry already knows, and it is not a
  result about chemistry. The group whose orbits a spectrum reports is Longuet-Higgins' molecular symmetry
  group: "the set of (i) all feasible permutations of the positions and spins of identical nuclei and (ii)
  all feasible permutation-inversions, which simultaneously invert the coordinates of all particles in the
  centre of mass" (*Mol. Phys.* 6:445, 1963), obtained from the complete nuclear permutation-inversion
  group by keeping only the elements he called feasible, since only those carry physically meaningful
  information. Feasibility is judged against the observation: ammonia and methyl fluoride both have
  inversion symmetry and only ammonia's is feasible. That is the whole of the admission test — a
  permutation with a properness flag, admitted when the process performing it is fast enough for the
  measurement — and it is in Bunker and Jensen's textbook. The naming of diastereotopic groups is Mislow
  and Raban (1967), the prochirality argument is Hanson (1966), and the enzymatic case is Ogston (1948).
  There is also an NMR-specific refinement in the same literature: the group applicable to NMR problems
  consists of the nuclear permutations that do not permute the NMR parameters, and may be larger or
  smaller than the molecular symmetry group. So the construction's device is prior art of long standing,
  and what the table demonstrates is fidelity, not discovery.
**links:** automorphism-is-physical · structure-elucidation · nmr-orbits.

### nmr-orbits
- The orbit prediction is already in the chemical literature, as a computation rather than a principle.
  Fullerene work from 1995 determines the topological equivalence classes of atoms and bonds in C₂₀–C₆₀ by
  generating all canonical labeled mapping trees, and states that "the number of atom classes and the
  number of atoms in each class gives important information on the number of 13C NMR signals and intensity
  patterns" (*J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci.*, 1995). Work from 2023 on coronene-based metal-organic
  frameworks develops "an efficient algorithm and computer code … to generate the automorphism
  partitioning of nuclei", and reads ¹³C signal counts and intensity ratios off it directly: for the
  hexagonal framework M₇₂S₂₂₈C₄₅₆, 43 signals with the intensity pattern given class by class
  (*ACS Omega*, 2023).
- So the field computes the automorphism partition when it wants a spectrum predicted, and discards it
  when it stores the molecule. The same group, in the same decade, is a published method and an absent
  datum.
- The prediction's limit is where the construction earns its keep. Protons that the graph identifies fall
  into three cases the textbooks separate: homotopic, exchanged by an automorphism of the constitution;
  enantiotopic, exchanged only by a reflection, so equivalent in an achiral medium and not in a chiral
  one; diastereotopic, not exchanged at all and generally giving distinct signals. Only the first is a
  graph automorphism. A signal count read off the bond graph is therefore an upper bound on the
  equivalences that hold, and a spectrum showing more signals than the graph predicts is not an anomaly.
- *Claude's gloss:* the group whose orbits a spectrum reports is the automorphism group of the oriented
  molecule, not of the graph — `OrAuto` in `chem/identity.cub`, an automorphism preserving handedness at
  every atom, with `or-orbit-refines` proving that its orbits refine the graph's, in that direction and
  not the other. That single refinement is the homotopic/diastereotopic distinction as a statement about
  groups, and it says what the demonstration must be: predicted counts from the oriented orbits, against
  published spectra, at every place the graph-only count is wrong. The enantiotopic case is the sharpest
  test, because there the right answer depends on the medium, which means the observation relation and not
  the molecule alone decides it.
- The named instance, with a published spectrum and sixty years of experimental work behind it: the two
  γ-methyl groups of valine. In the bond graph they are exchanged by an automorphism — both are CH₃ on
  the same Cβ — so a graph-orbit count puts them in one class and predicts one resonance. They are
  diastereotopic, because Cα is a stereocentre, and in free valine they appear as two doublets, at about
  0.99 and 1.04 ppm. The field did not merely observe the splitting; it built a chemistry to resolve it.
  Neri, Otting and Wüthrich, "¹H and ¹³C NMR chemical shifts of the diastereotopic methyl groups of valyl
  and leucyl residues in peptides and proteins" (*Tetrahedron* 46:3287, 1990), obtained stereospecific
  assignments by biosynthetically directed fractional ¹³C labelling — a mixture of fully labelled and
  unlabelled glucose as sole carbon source, with the known stereoselectivity of valine and leucine
  biosynthesis doing the discrimination — and deposited them as BMRB entry 486, 28 ¹³C and 84 ¹H shifts.
  Later patents cover reagents that protonate the pro-S γ2 methyl and leave only the pro-R γ1 observable.
  Williamson and colleagues (*FEBS Lett.* 302:185, 1992) calculated shifts for diastereotopic groups and
  reported stereospecific assignments correct about 80% of the time.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, computed:* valine is now written in `chem/identity.cub` as what it is —
  nineteen atoms as a tree, each atom's neighbour towards the carboxyl carbon with the order of that bond,
  the elements, and four generators of its bond-graph symmetries: the two methyl rotations, the exchange of
  the two methyl groups, and the exchange of the amine hydrogens. The machine then computes the proton
  signal count twice, by closing the orbits under the generators and counting the classes among the eleven
  hydrogens. From the bond graph: five (`vln-signals-graph`) — hydroxyl, α, the amine pair as one, β, and
  all six methyl hydrogens as one, because the exchange of the two methyls is a symmetry of the
  connectivity. From the generators that also preserve the arrangement at every carbon, which is the same
  list without that exchange: six (`vln-signals-conf`). `vln-counts-differ` proves the two counts are
  unequal. The published spectrum shows six, with the two γ-methyl doublets at about 0.99 and 1.04 ppm. So
  the standard representation of valine determines a count that its own spectra refute, the correct count
  follows from adding the arrangements, and the whole difference is one group element.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the table:* three more compounds were then written the same way, and the
  three disagreements have three different causes. Leucine carries two reversals where valine carried one
  — the two δ-methyls at the γ carbon and the two β hydrogens — so its counts are six from the graph and
  eight from the configured molecule (`lu-signals-graph`, `lu-signals-conf`, `lu-counts-differ`).
  Isoleucine has two stereocentres and *fewer* graph symmetries than leucine, because its two methyls sit
  on different kinds of carbon and the connectivity already separates them; its disagreement is at the γ
  methylene alone, seven against eight (`il-counts-differ`), and the random-coil table resolves that pair
  by a wide margin, 1.48 and 1.19 ppm. So the disagreement does not track the number of stereocentres,
  and threonine — two stereocentres, no disagreement — is in the table to make that explicit.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the enantiotopic case:* glycerol is the compound for which there is no
  single correct count. Its central carbon carries a hydrogen, a hydroxyl and two identical arms, so the
  molecule is achiral and its only symmetry operation is the reflection exchanging the arms; the arms are
  enantiotopic and the two hydrogens within each methylene are diastereotopic. Three groups of operations
  act, and three counts are computed from the same two definitions: four from the bond graph, five in an
  achiral medium, eight in a chiral one, with `gl-graph-not-achiral`, `gl-achiral-not-chiral` and
  `gl-graph-not-chiral` refuting each equality. Five is what the ordinary spectrum in water shows — two
  methylene classes near 3.55 and 3.65 ppm and the central hydrogen near 3.78 — and eight is what a
  chiral solvating agent shows, with nothing changed about the molecule. The signal count is therefore not
  a property of a molecule but of a molecule together with what surrounds it, which is the observation
  relation deciding the count and not the structure. The field acts on this already when it adds a chiral
  solvating or derivatizing agent, and glycerol kinase makes the same separation with no spectrometer, by
  being a chiral environment itself. Citric acid is the same shape of case with all three counts distinct
  and the middle one observed: one class from the graph, two in water — the AB quartet at 2.54 and 2.66
  ppm that is the standard teaching example — and four in a chiral medium.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, peaks against classes:* the leucine case forced a distinction the valine
  case did not. Leucine's two β hydrogens are two classes, and the random-coil table gives them one value,
  1.65 ppm, while the γ hydrogen sits at 1.64. So a molecule with eight classes shows seven peaks.
  `peak-count` is now a definition of its own — the distinct shift values over a list of atoms — and
  `lu-shift` carries the measured shifts with proofs that it respects each configured generator, so
  `lu-peaks-below-classes` is the statement that the peak count falls strictly below the class count on a
  shift assignment that respects the symmetry. Accidental equivalence of shift is not identity of class.
  The consequence runs the opposite way from the valine case: there the graph predicted too few classes
  and the spectrum showed the true number; here the configured molecule predicts the true number and the
  spectrum shows fewer peaks. A count read off a spectrum is a lower bound on the classes, and any
  procedure that reads structural equivalence off observed equivalence — which is what automatic
  assignment does — infers a symmetry the molecule does not have.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, and which way the evidence points:* `vln-shift-coincident` is valine's
  spectrum in the case where its two methyls coincide, as they do at lower field or in a different
  solvent, and as diastereotopic groups do outright in many molecules. It is proved to respect the methyl
  exchange as well as the three configured generators, and its peak count is five — exactly what the bond
  graph predicts (`vln-coincident-matches-graph`), while differing from the configured count
  (`vln-coincident-not-conf`). So a five-peak spectrum of valine is consistent with both carriers, and a
  count read off a spectrum cannot choose between them. Only a resolved spectrum separates them, and
  whether a spectrum resolves is a property of the field strength and the line width. That is why the
  assignments took isotope labelling rather than better fitting.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the table at scale:* the four worked compounds each cost a type, a rank, an
  element table, a bond table and a generator written out atom by atom — the right form for a case to be
  read and checked by hand, the wrong form for a table. A compound is now data over the atoms 0 to n−1:
  `nat-xch` exchanges two lists of atoms position by position, `nat-cyc` cycles one, every symmetry in
  these molecules is built from those two, and `signals` computes the class count. Valine is encoded both
  ways and the two encodings are proved to agree (`num-val-agrees-graph`, `num-val-agrees-conf`), which is
  the cross-check the numeric form had to pass. Counts are taken over carbon-bound hydrogens only: in
  water an amino acid is a zwitterion, its carboxyl carries no hydrogen and its ammonium's three are
  related by a rotation about the C–N bond, which is a proper operation and so a symmetry under both
  readings — nothing about the exchangeable hydrogens distinguishes the carriers, and in deuterated
  solvent they are not observed.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what the table says:* `nmr-table` holds fourteen compounds — valine,
  leucine, isoleucine, glycerol, alanine, glycine, serine, threonine, phenylalanine, ethanol, 2-propanol,
  2-butanol, toluene, citric acid — each row carrying the graph count and the configured count, both
  computed from that compound's encoding, against the number of peaks its published spectrum reports.
  Two numbers come out of it by computation. The bond graph gives the wrong count for **eight of the
  fourteen**: valine, leucine, isoleucine, glycerol, serine, phenylalanine, 2-butanol, citric acid
  (`nmr-graph-wrong`), and `nmr-graph-not-sound` refutes its soundness over the table. It is right for the
  other six, where the molecule's only symmetries are proper — methyl rotations, ring rotations — or where
  the achiral solvent supplies the improper one anyway. The failure is therefore neither sporadic nor
  universal: it is exactly the failure to distinguish a rotation from a reflection, and it bites wherever
  a molecule has a diastereotopic pair. The configured count differs from the reported peak count for
  **two of the fourteen** (`nmr-med-wrong`), leucine and toluene, both in the same direction and both
  proved to be accidental coincidences of shift — toluene's ring hydrogens next to the methyl resonate at
  7.17 with the one opposite it, giving three peaks for four classes (`num-tol-peaks-below`). In the other
  twelve the computed count is the reported count. The compounds are ordinary: three of the twenty amino
  acids, the alcohol in every solvent cabinet, the acid in every citrus fruit.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the setting layer:* every count above was taken under a list of operations
  chosen by hand, with the choice justified in a comment — these are proper, that one is a reflection, that
  one no medium supplies. That justification now sits in the construction. A `BMotion` is a permutation with its rate as a
  function of the bath and whether it is proper; a `Bath` is the temperature, the rate the observation
  resolves, and the solution — a list of `Dissolved` constituents, each a tag naming the substance, the
  order of magnitude of its concentration, and whether it is present in excess over its mirror image;
  `bm-admitted` is a test with two clauses — fast enough in this bath to average, and proper unless the
  medium cannot tell the molecule from its mirror image — and `count-bath` counts the classes of a
  `Material` under exactly the operations admitted. Every count above is a value of that one function.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the timescale:* two more compounds, which the handedness clause cannot
  explain at all. Cyclohexane's chair has a three-fold and three two-fold rotations, fast and proper, and
  its chair flip exchanges every axial hydrogen with the equatorial one on its own carbon — also proper,
  with a barrier of 10.3–10.8 kcal/mol, which is about 10⁵ s⁻¹ at room temperature and a few per second
  near 190 K. So: one class warm, two cold, with nothing about the molecule changed
  (`m-cyh-room`, `m-cyh-cold`, `m-temperature-decides`). The measurement is the one on
  cyclohexane-d₁₁: a single sharp peak at 1.43 ppm which broadens from about −60 °C and, below −89 °C,
  resolves into two, equatorial 1.62 and axial 1.14. N,N-dimethylformamide is the same shape of case with
  the opposite moral. The rotation about its amide C–N bond, which would exchange its two methyls, has a
  free energy of activation of 19–21 kcal/mol in solution (21.0 by total line-shape analysis in CCl₄),
  about 10⁻³ s⁻¹ at room temperature and about 30 s⁻¹ at 400 K. Three classes warm — the two methyl
  singlets at 2.97 and 2.88 and the formyl hydrogen at 7.95 — and two hot. The bond graph's reading of DMF
  is therefore right above its coalescence temperature and wrong below it, which is not a property a
  representation of a molecule can have.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, why the two coalescences differ:* the resolving rate belongs to the setting,
  not to the molecule, and that is the whole explanation of why coalescence comes near 120 °C in DMF and
  near −64 °C in cyclohexane with barriers only a factor of two apart. Cyclohexane's axial and equatorial
  shifts differ by 0.48 ppm, about 144 Hz at 300 MHz; DMF's methyls by 0.09 ppm, about 27 Hz. An order of
  magnitude in the instrument's resolving rate, given per case in `bath-room`, `bath-cold` and `bath-hot`, since it belongs to the pair of shifts and the instrument. A molecule has one list of motions, not one per temperature: each rate is a function of the bath (`brate-flat`, `brate-step`, `brate-catalysed`), with the crossing where the measurement puts it rather than from Eyring's relation, which wants a logarithm the core does not have.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what the bond graph actually claims:* glycerol re-expressed in the layer
  shows the medium case and the timescale case to be one test with two clauses. Its single operation is
  the improper arm exchange, and `m-glycerol-claimed` writes out what a graph-automorphism count asserts
  in addition: that there exist fast proper motions exchanging the two hydrogens within each methylene.
  No such motion exists — performing it means breaking bonds. So the defect, stated in these terms, is not
  a missing field in a file format. It is a false claim about what the molecule does. And
  `m-glc-claimed-chiral` is the cautionary case: the claimed motions in a chiral medium give three,
  which is the correct count in an achiral one — two errors cancelling, the invented proper motions
  identifying what should be separate and the refused improper one separating what should be identified.
  A count that agrees is not evidence that a representation is right, so the six compounds the bond graph
  gets right in the table are not six it gets right for the right reason.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the four mechanisms:* `four-mechanisms` collects the whole result as one
  object holding four refutations about named compounds. A signal count can differ from another for four
  reasons and no more: a representation admitting permutations no motion performs (the table, eight of
  fourteen); the handedness of the medium (glycerol); the rate of a motion against the rate the
  observation resolves (cyclohexane); accidental coincidence of shift (leucine). `bath-is-irredundant` then
  proves nothing in the bath is spare, and each pair of baths it compares differs in exactly one quantity:
  cyclohexane's two differ in temperature, glycerol's in whether the same agent at the same concentration
  is one enantiomer or both, glucose's in how much base. A temperature, an enantiomeric excess and a
  concentration, none of them a property of any molecule in the bath. It is also the answer to the
  obvious objection, that the fix is to record the stereochemistry and be done: no amount of
  stereochemistry in the record determines cyclohexane's count, and no amount of kinetics determines
  glycerol's. The first is a defect in
  a representation and can be corrected. The other three are not defects — they are what it means for an
  observation to be of a molecule in a situation — and no correction to a structure field reaches them. A
  record that stores a spectrum against a structure, with no field for the medium, the temperature or the
  instrument, is storing an observation where the thing that determines it is the observation relation.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, reports that carry their conditions:* the count being a function of the
  compound and the condition is a statement about what a record has to hold, and the construction now
  holds it. A `Material` is how many atoms, what motions, and which atoms the observation counts — the
  middle one being the part a structure file does not have, which holds the first and the third and calls
  it the molecule. `Bath` is the temperature, the resolving rate and everything dissolved. `count-bath` is the function, and it is total.
  A `BReport` carries the material, the bath, the warrant the number rests on with an inhabitant of
  it, and the value; `br-cyh-room` and `br-cyh-cold` are the two cyclohexane measurements written
  that way, each warranted by its own computation.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, glucose, and why the medium is not a flag on a solvent bottle:* the case
  that forced the general form is a sugar in water. The pyranose ring opens and closes and the anomeric
  hydroxyl comes back on either face, so D-glucose in solution is about 36% α and 64% β at 25 °C — the
  ratio behind the specific rotations, +112° for the α anomer, +19° for the β, +52.7° for the equilibrium
  solution, which is the measurement mutarotation was found by (Dubrunfaut, 1846). The rate is about 0.015
  min⁻¹ at 26 °C, which is 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹: an order of magnitude of zero, far below anything a
  spectrometer averages, so the two anomeric protons stay separate — a doublet at 5.22 ppm for α and one
  at 4.64 for β, their integrals in the ratio of the composition. That is how anomerization is followed in
  real time, and how α-D-glucose serves as an MRS tracer in vivo at 5.22 ppm. What makes the exchange fast
  is not the temperature: heating to 90 °C leaves the anomers resolved. It is a catalyst — base, or glucose
  isomerase — which is in the medium. So a rate is a function of the whole situation, and the exchange here
  is not an automorphism of a molecule at all but a path between two forms, modelled as the interconversion
  class carrying both forms' anomeric protons (`m-glucose`, `m-glu-water`, `m-glu-trace`, `m-glu-base`,
  `m-concentration-decides`).
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what glucose does to the question "how many substances":* whether D-glucose
  is one substance or two is a question about a rate against an observation, and both answers are correct
  under their own situations. The registry gives all three answers at once — an identifier for the α
  anomer, one for the β, one for D-glucose — and the relation between them, that the third is the
  interconversion class of the first two with a composition determined by the temperature and the solvent,
  is in none of them. `glu-fraction` carries that composition as what it is: a function of the condition
  and not of either structure. `Mix` and `mix-frac-differs` say the same for UVCB substances; a sugar in
  water is the same statement in a case nobody would call a UVCB.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the medium as chemistry, and the racemate that separates nothing:* the
  handedness of the medium is not a flag anyone measures; what is measured is what is dissolved. So the
  bath holds a solution and the handedness is computed from it: `sol-chiral` is true exactly when some
  constituent is in excess over its mirror image, and `excess-honest` is the condition tying that field to
  the graph — a constituent may be marked in excess only if its own record carries no improper symmetry
  (`material-chiral`, `any-improper-b`), so a record marking water as in excess is not wrong about water's
  concentration but claiming a handedness water does not have. Achirality is therefore carried explicitly:
  for water and for dimethylformamide a mirror plane acting as the identity on the atoms, for cyclohexane
  the chair's inversion centre, which maps axial hydrogens to axial and so changes no count in an achiral
  bath. Water is a material in the graph in its own right, since a solvent is a substance; its own proton
  count is one, which is the spectrum of water. Then the result: glycerol in water, three classes; in water
  with one enantiomer of Pirkle's alcohol, 1-(9-anthryl)-2,2,2-trifluoroethanol, five; with the same agent
  at the same concentration as a racemate, three again, and that is exactly the plain-water count.
  `m-handedness-decides` refutes the equality of the last two. The chiral substance is present and
  dissolved and the enantiotopic arms are not separated; what separates them is the excess. That is the
  statement Pirkle's 1966 title makes — the nonequivalence of physical properties of enantiomers in
  optically active solvents, *J. Am. Chem. Soc.* 88:1837 — and an optically inactive solution of an
  optically active compound is not an optically active solvent.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the count that turns on pH:* a rate reads a concentration, not a flag.
  `brate-catalysed` names the catalyst per motion and adds its dissolved amount to the base rate, which is
  what a first-order dependence looks like with both read as orders of magnitude. Glucose in plain water:
  two anomeric signals. With hydroxide present in trace: still two — the catalyst is in the bottle and the
  anomers stay separate, because the rate it buys is below what the experiment resolves. With enough of it:
  one. So the count turns on a concentration, and in water that quantity is the pH. The middle case is the
  one a presence flag could not express and the ordinary one: a substance is in the solution and does
  nothing observable. This is also where the cycle in the obvious design shows itself — a mixture of full
  records would hold substances, a substance holds motions, and a motion's rate would take an argument
  holding the mixture. What a rate needs of the medium is the references and not the records, so the
  solution names its constituents by tag and the records stay in the graph.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the cost, derived:* `count-steps` carries the cost of counting, from the
  definitions rather than from a clock. With n the atom bound, g the number of motions, h the atoms counted
  and s the constituents dissolved: admission is g·s comparisons, since a rate reading a concentration
  scans the solution; the orbit closure runs n iterations over a list of at most n atoms, each applying g
  generators to every atom reached and then reducing (1+g)·m atoms by `mem-by` on each tail, which sums to
  n³g² comparisons; `rep-fn` adds n for the minimum; `class-count` repeats that for each counted atom and
  nubs the h representatives, h². So g·s + h·n³g² + h², reached only by a material whose motions carry
  every atom to every atom. Every quantity in the bound is local to one material and one bath: no step of
  the counting holds a corpus or any other whole, and its peak store is one orbit, at most n atoms.
  Deriving it found a defect. `min-by` named its recursive call twice, once in the scrutinee and once in
  the branch, so its step count satisfied T(m) = 2T(m−1) + 1 and picking the least of a nineteen-atom orbit
  cost half a million steps, once per counted atom. Folded with the running least as the default it visits
  each element once, Θ(m), and the two forms agree wherever the default is itself in the list — which is
  every use here, since a representative is taken with its own atom as the default and an atom is in its
  own orbit.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the sharpening of the disagreement theorem:* `reports-disagree-no-function`
  convicted every disagreement alike, and so convicted a variable-temperature series along with a
  contradiction. `breports-same-bath-disagree` puts the bath in the hypothesis: two reports must agree
  on their bath before their disagreement refutes a function of the material and the bath, so a
  variable-temperature series, a titration and a chiral-solvent experiment are no longer convicted
  alongside a contradiction. The
  Classification and Labelling Inventory's notifications are of that kind — one substance, one hazard
  class, one set of criteria, two answers. `br-cyh-consistent` is the other side, and it is the one
  the earlier theorem got wrong: two reports on one compound whose values disagree, together with one
  function agreeing with both, the function being the count itself. A record that cannot tell these two
  cases apart is a record whose disagreements cannot be read at all, in either direction — it will call a
  temperature series a contradiction and a contradiction a difference of conditions.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the table at thirty compounds:* a compound is now a row of data —
  how many atoms, the generators of its bond-graph symmetries, those of them that also preserve the
  arrangement at every carbon, the hydrogens an observation counts, and the number of peaks its published
  spectrum reports — and both counts are computed inside the tally (`Compd`, `compds`,
  `tally-graph-wrong-of`, `tally-conf-wrong-of`). Adding a compound costs a line, so the table now covers
  every proteinogenic amino acid together with glycerol, ethanol, 2-propanol, 2-butanol, toluene, citric
  acid, and four drugs: ibuprofen, aspirin, paracetamol and caffeine. The amino acids take their peak counts from one source so the rows are comparable: the
  standard random-coil table in GGXA peptides at pH 7.0 and 35 °C (Wüthrich 1986). That corrects serine's
  row from 3 to 2 — the table gives its β methylene one value, 3.88, where the free amino acid in water
  resolves 3.94 and 3.98 — and the basis is stated for the table rather than assumed per row, since a
  count read from a free amino acid in one row and from a peptide in another is not comparable.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what thirty rows say:* the bond graph gives the wrong count for **twenty-one
  of the thirty** (`compds-graph-wrong`), so **fourteen of the twenty amino acids are counted wrongly
  by the representation the field stores them in**. The nine it gets right are alanine, glycine, threonine,
  ethanol, 2-propanol, toluene, aspirin, paracetamol and caffeine, whose only symmetries are proper or
  whose improper one an achiral solvent supplies anyway — three of those nine being the drugs without a
  stereocentre, while the fourth drug, the one with a stereocentre, is counted wrongly. The configured
  count differs from the reported peaks for **ten** (`compds-conf-wrong`), always with fewer peaks than
  classes, and the pattern is geometric: the coincident
  pairs are the ones far from the stereocentre — lysine's γ, δ and ε methylenes each give one value while
  its β pair gives two; methionine's γ pair coincides and its β pair does not; proline's γ pair coincides
  while its β and δ resolve; tryptophan's two coincident resonances are ring positions 2 and 6. The
  splitting is real and decays with distance, which is why the class count is the prediction and the peak
  count is what an instrument of a given field strength returns.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, ibuprofen, and a count that agrees for the wrong reason:* ibuprofen has nine
  classes — two ring, the α hydrogen, its methyl, the two benzylic hydrogens separately, the isopropyl
  hydrogen, and the two isobutyl methyls separately — and seven peaks: 7.21, 7.09, 3.70, 2.44, 1.84, 1.49,
  0.89 in deuterochloroform. The two coincidences are the benzylic pair, one doublet of two hydrogens at
  2.44, and the isobutyl methyls, one doublet of six at 0.89, both remote from the stereocentre, which is
  where coincidences are. And seven is also what the bond graph predicts. So on ibuprofen the discarded
  representation agrees with the spectrum by cancelling two of its own errors against two accidental
  coincidences of shift — the same shape as `m-glc-claimed-chiral` above, now on a drug sold by the tonne.
  A count that agrees is not evidence that a representation is right, and a validation that scores a
  representation by how often its counts match published peak counts would score this one as correct here.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the two inequalities:* over all thirty rows and without exception, the
  bond graph's count never exceeds the configured count (`compds-graph-below`) — which is
  `or-orbit-refines` computed on real molecules rather than proved abstractly — and no spectrum reports
  more peaks than the molecule has classes (`compds-peaks-below`). The configured count is the quantity
  the other two are bounded by, which is the statement that it is the one the molecule has. A single row
  with more peaks than classes would refute the construction outright. `table-result` holds the five
  statements together.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, tartaric acid, where one bit decides:* the sharpest form of the defect, on
  the compound stereochemistry began with. (R,R)-tartaric acid and meso-tartaric acid have the same atoms,
  the same bonds, the same constitution, the same formula and the same symmetry permutation — the exchange
  of their two halves. They differ in whether that permutation is a rotation or a reflection: in the (R,R)
  acid the two methine hydrogens are homotopic, in the meso acid enantiotopic. `m-tartaric-rr` and
  `m-tartaric-meso` differ in exactly one boolean, and `material-chiral` computes the first chiral and the
  second achiral from that bit alone. In water both show one methine resonance — the observed singlet near
  4.5 ppm — and `tar-agree-in-water` proves the counts equal; in a bath holding one enantiomer of Pirkle's
  alcohol the (R,R) acid still shows one and the meso acid shows two, which `tar-differ-in-chiral` refutes
  the equality of. The chiral-bath count for the meso acid is stated as a prediction of the construction
  rather than a figure read off a published spectrum. So a representation that drops properness has not
  lost a detail about tartaric acid: it has identified two substances that Pasteur separated by hand in
  1848, sorting the two crystal forms of sodium ammonium tartrate under a lens — the first separation of
  enantiomers ever made, with his own handedness as the chiral agent.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, Ogston, and the inference this whole section refutes:* the same mistake was
  made and corrected in 1948. Wood and colleagues introduced isotopic carbon as carbon dioxide with
  pyruvate, found ketoglutarate labelled only in the carboxy group next to the keto group, and excluded
  citrate as an intermediate of the cycle: citrate is symmetric, the product is not, so the intermediate
  cannot have been citrate. Ogston refuted the inference the same year (*Nature* 162, 963, 18 December
  1948): an asymmetric enzyme attacking a symmetric compound can distinguish between its identical groups,
  so the label's position says nothing against a symmetric intermediate. Aconitase acts on the arm of
  citrate derived from oxaloacetate every time, and the three-point attachment he proposed is how it tells
  the arms apart. The inference is precisely the one the setting layer refutes — it treats an observation
  of a molecule in an environment as a function of the molecule alone — and the construction states
  exactly what the experiment could not decide. `m-citrate` has one operation, the improper exchange of its
  two carboxymethyl arms; `m-citrate-asym` has the same atoms and no operation relating them, which is what
  citrate would have had to be had the inference been sound. In water the first shows two classes of
  methylene hydrogen (the AB quartet at 2.54 and 2.66 ppm) and the second four; in a chiral environment
  both show four. `ogston-chiral-agrees` proves they agree there and `ogston-achiral-separates` refutes
  their agreement in water, so an experiment carried out in a chiral environment cannot distinguish them —
  and the tracer experiment was carried out in an enzyme. The shape reaches past the one experiment: any
  inference from an observed asymmetry to an asymmetric substrate has it, and metabolism is full of them —
  a label in one position, a product with one configuration, an isotope effect at one site. None of them
  bounds the symmetry of what went in. In these terms the three-point attachment is the enzyme supplying
  an improper operation's refusal: the role Pirkle's alcohol plays in a tube, played by a protein in a
  cell.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the construction as a diagnostic:* what a knowledge graph would actually
  run. Given a record — atoms, bond-graph symmetries, the symmetries that also preserve the arrangement at
  every carbon, the counted hydrogens, the peaks reported — there are four cases and which one a record is
  in is decidable from the record (`diagnose`): the three numbers agree; the bond graph's count is wrong
  and the configured count is the reported peak count; the two counts agree and the peaks are fewer; or
  both at once. Over the thirty compounds the cases come out **eight, twelve, one and nine**, and
  `diagnosis-partitions` proves they exhaust the table. The twelve are the plain case the field's records
  are in, where the construction is not a refinement of the record but the correction of it. The one is
  toluene, where nothing about the representation is at fault and nothing about the construction fixes it —
  it is the instrument. The nine are the dangerous case: the representation is wrong and a coincidence
  hides part of the difference, so a validation that scores a representation by how often its counts match
  published peak counts gets partial credit for the wrong representation, and on ibuprofen full credit.
  The number that matters for a record's trustworthiness is the twelve plus the nine: twenty-one of thirty
  compounds whose stored count is wrong.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the dose, which is where this ends up mattering:* a hazard stored against a
  substance is read as the hazard of what is administered, and for a substance whose forms interconvert in
  the body that reading fails in the one direction that kills. Thalidomide's forms invert at a measured
  half-life in human serum of 22 minutes from the (S) form and 33 from the (R), at pH 7.4 and 37 °C
  (`td-inv`), and the (S) form is the teratogen. `form-hazard` is the hazard a record carries for a pure
  form; `class-hazard` is the hazard of what is present once the forms have had time to interconvert — the
  worst in the interconversion class, with the rate against the window deciding whether the class is one
  form or two, which is `ic-within` applied to a hazard instead of a spectrum. The (R) form's own hazard is
  nothing; over a window of a day against an inversion half-life of 33 minutes the hazard of what is
  present is the (S) form's, and the window is the one that matters for a teratogen, since the susceptible
  period of limb development is days. `dose-hazard-not-determined` refutes the equality of the two numbers,
  computed from the same form with the same hazard function. Nothing about the (R) form changed between
  them except how long it was in a body. A record storing a hazard against a substance identifier, with no
  field for the interconversion and no field for the window, cannot express the difference, and the reading
  it invites is the one that says the (R) form is safe.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the pH-rate profile, computed:* the rate of a change in water is the sum of
  an uncatalysed term, an acid-catalysed term in the hydronium concentration and a base-catalysed term in
  the hydroxide concentration — k₀ + k_H[H⁺] + k_OH[OH⁻] — and `brate-acid-base` reads two tagged amounts
  out of the solution to carry it. Glucose's anomerization is catalysed from both ends, which is why
  mutarotation has a pH-rate profile with a minimum rather than a threshold, and the counts come out two in
  neutral water, one in acid, one in base (`ph-neutral`, `ph-acid`, `ph-base`,
  `ph-profile-has-a-minimum`): the same molecule, the same instrument, the same temperature, and the number
  of signals falling at either end of the acidity scale. A record with a field for the pH can hold this; a
  record with a field for the structure cannot, and a record with a boolean for whether a catalyst is
  present gets the middle case wrong in both directions.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, the racemate's affinity:* the pharmacological twin of the dose statement, and
  one line. A drug sold as a racemate has one record and two substances in it, and their potencies differ —
  for propranolol the (S) enantiomer is about a hundredfold the β-blocker the (R) is. So no single number
  stored against the racemate's record is the affinity of both enantiomers, whatever number it is and
  however it was measured (`racemate-affinity-not-determined`). A record carrying one affinity per
  identifier therefore carries, for every racemic drug, a number that is the affinity of neither
  constituent — and the apparent value a competition assay returns on the mixture is a third number again,
  lying between them and belonging to the mixture at that composition.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, one shape:* the signal count is not a property of a molecule; the atom map
  is not a property of a pair of molecules; the hazard is not a property of a substance. Each is a property
  of the thing together with what surrounds it and for how long, and the record has fields for the thing
  alone. That is the whole of what this section establishes, and every piece of it is a computation on a
  named compound with a published measurement.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what of this is known, checked rather than assumed:* all of the chemistry,
  and the central device. Beyond Longuet-Higgins' feasible permutation-inversion group (`nmr-prediction`),
  the specific failures are named in the field's own current literature. The descriptor used for shift
  prediction carries no stereochemistry — "since the HOSE code disregards stereochemistry, for
  stereochemically different atoms, the same shifts will be predicted" — and the stereo-aware extension
  that fixes it was built and put into nmrshiftdb2, demonstrated on a compound with a chiral centre and
  diastereotopic methyl groups (*ACS Omega*, 2019). Work from 2026 on ¹³C prediction reports the same thing
  structurally: a recurrent mismatch between topological equivalence and the real chemical environment,
  with 2D topological equivalence collapsing chemically distinct environments, as the motivation for
  explicit 3D representations. On the reaction side the degeneracy is formalised: atom-to-atom maps are
  determined only up to automorphisms of the reactant and product graphs, two chemically equivalent
  hydrogens yield two alternative maps under enumeration, and the equivalence of atom maps is proved
  equivalent to isomorphism of labelled auxiliary graphs (*Symmetry* 16:1217, 2024, with the imaginary
  transition state formalism; and the graph-transformation treatment of chemistry in the double-pushout
  framework, Andersen, Flamm, Merkle and Stadler). That literature also records the converse case this
  construction needs — atoms that are chemically equivalent although they lie in no common automorphism
  orbit, which is the tautomer situation. And the molecule-against-substance confusion is named in the
  chemical ontology literature: using a connection table as a chemical identifier "leads to a fundamental
  ontological confusion between the universal 'molecule' and a 'real world' bulk substance", while
  information systems ascribe a substance's physicochemical property to the molecule (ChemAxiom, 2009),
  with the same literature observing that the field has been slow to adopt formal ontology.
- *Claude's gloss, 2026-11-18, what is left:* not the facts and not the device. What the search did not
  turn up is a machine-checked artifact in which these distinctions are definitions and their failures are
  refutations, in one carrier that also holds the digests, the warrants and the scopes — formalisation of
  chemistry in a proof assistant exists and is aimed elsewhere, at thermodynamics, adsorption and
  kinematics (Lean, arXiv:2210.12150). So the claim this section can make is about a carrier and its
  checks, not about chemistry, and the compounds are evidence that the carrier is faithful to what the
  field established between 1948 and 1967 and has been repairing in its tooling since.
- *Claude's gloss on the record:* both Neri papers are in play and they are different papers. The method
  is Neri, Szyperski, Otting, Senn and Wüthrich, *Biochemistry* 28:7510–7516 (1989), biosynthetically
  directed fractional ¹³C labelling applied to the fourteen valyl and leucyl residues of the 434 repressor
  DNA-binding domain, deposited as BMRB entry 195. The shift compilation for valyl and leucyl residues in
  peptides and proteins is Neri, Otting and Wüthrich, *Tetrahedron* 46:3287–3296 (1990), deposited as BMRB
  entry 486 against repressor P22 c2. An earlier gloss here cited only the second; the definitions in
  `chem/identity.cub` now cite both, the method for the assignment and the compilation for the shifts.
- *Claude's gloss:* so for valine the distinction between the two methyls is the subject of a labelling
  chemistry, a calculation literature and a public deposition, and the molecule's canonical identifier
  carries none of it — the InChI of valine does not distinguish γ1 from γ2, because the automorphism that
  would have to be broken is not in the representation. The construction's worked case
  (`the-construction`) is this molecule's pattern in its smallest form, proved both ways; valine is where
  it meets a recorded spectrum.
