### pfas-scope
- The largest live instance of a scope defined by structure is PFAS, and the numbers show what the
  definition does. The OECD's revised 2021 definition reads: "PFASs are defined as fluorinated substances
  that contain at least one fully fluorinated methyl or methylene carbon atom (without any H/Cl/Br/I atom
  attached to it)" — so, with a few noted exceptions, any chemical carrying a –CF₃ or a –CF₂– group. It
  replaced a narrower formulation and was adopted into REACH's restriction scope and by ECHA.
- Applied to a corpus, that criterion reclassifies the world. Schymanski and colleagues ran it over
  PubChem ("PFAS in PubChem: 7 Million and Growing", *Environ. Sci. Technol.*, 2023): of 116 million
  compounds, over 7 million satisfy it, "several orders of magnitude higher than previously" — against the
  OECD's own curated PFAS collection of about 4,700 and the EPA's PFASMASTER list of about 10,000. The
  OECD's cosmetics report records its working list growing from 6,330 to 14,735 within a year.
- The derived limits moved as far as the scope did, and in the other direction. The EPA's 2016 lifetime
  health advisory for PFOA and PFOS combined was 70 ng/L; its 2022 interim updated advisories are
  0.004 ng/L for PFOA and 0.02 ng/L for PFOS; the April 2024 National Primary Drinking Water Regulation
  sets an enforceable maximum contaminant level of 4 ng/L for each. The EU's drinking-water directive
  instead limits total PFAS to 0.5 µg/L with 20 named PFAS at 0.1 µg/L each. One hazard, four numbers
  spanning four orders of magnitude, each derived from a model of the same underlying evidence.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is `markush` and `substantially-similar` with the consequences attached, and the
  construction's two statements apply exactly. `scope-respects` says a scope cannot distinguish identical
  molecules, so a structural criterion is a legitimate scope — the –CF₂– test is decidable and
  reproducible, which is more than "substantially similar" ever managed. `HazardDetermined` is the claim
  that membership determines the hazard, and it is the claim nobody has established for seven million
  members on evidence from a handful; `ra-transports` is what a class-wide regulation would need, and
  `ra-via-property` names the legitimate version — the property that the hazard depends on is persistence
  and mobility, which is why the class argument is made on the carbon–fluorine bond rather than on
  resemblance. The limits are `Derived` values: 70, 0.004, 0.02, 4 and 500 ng/L are not disagreements
  about a measurement but outputs of different models over the same reports, and none of them is published
  with the dependency attached in a form that lets a reader recompute it when one input changes. Both
  halves of the construction are needed at once here, and the field has the first (a decidable scope) and
  not the second (the warrant for transporting the hazard across it).
