### classification-disagreement
- The hazard classification of a single substance is, in the European record, a multi-valued entry. ECHA's
  Classification and Labelling Inventory holds the harmonised classifications of Annex VI to CLP, which
  are legally binding, together with the self-classifications that manufacturers and importers notify —
  and the agency states plainly that it "does not verify the accuracy of the data submitted by industry".
  Where notifiers differ, the inventory shows it: entries for the same substance are grouped by numerical
  identifier, "so that different classifications and labellings for the same substance will be visible if
  different notifiers or registrants have arrived at different classifications".
- The law's response to a disagreement is a duty to converse. CLP Article 41 provides that where a
  notification produces an entry differing from another for the same substance, the notifiers and
  registrants "shall make every effort to come to an agreed entry". There is no criterion supplied for
  which entry is right; harmonisation is a separate procedure, triggered among other ways by someone
  discovering that different entries exist and having reason to think the matter should be settled.
- The agency has now made the disagreement a published statistic: the rebuilt inventory groups and ranks
  self-classifications "by the percentage of notifiers in agreement", so a reader sees how many companies
  chose each classification of the same substance. The European Parliament's briefing on the CLP revision
  names the problem in the same words — diverging notified self-classifications, and diverging
  classifications for the same substance in the inventory.
- Downstream, propagation is manual. ECHA's own guidance for anyone classifying a mixture tells them what
  to do if the classifications they receive from suppliers for the same substance disagree, or disagree
  with the inventory: verify the substance identity and properties, contact the suppliers, and if the
  discrepancy is not explained, escalate.
- *Claude's gloss:* a hazard treated as a function of the substance presupposes one value per substance,
  and the record holds several, so the function is not defined — which is `identity-authority` at the
  level of the hazard rather than the identifier. The construction's reading is exact: each notified
  classification is a `Report` whose warrant is a notifier's assessment, the harmonised entry is a
  distinguished authority's `Report` that overrides, and the percentage-in-agreement that the new
  interface displays is a statistic over warrants, which is the right object to display and the wrong one
  to decide by. What is missing is the dependency: a mixture classified from a substance's entry inherits
  it, and when a harmonisation changes that entry, `der-retracted` is the proposition that would
  enumerate every mixture classification now standing on a retracted premise. The guidance tells the
  downstream user to telephone their supplier, which is that enumeration carried out by hand, one link at
  a time, with no record of who was called.
**links:** identity-authority · registrant-practice · regulatory-identity · read-across ·
retraction-propagation · the-construction.

### read-across
- The inference this whole document is about — similar structure, therefore similar hazard — is a formal
  regulatory instrument, and its success rate has now been measured across the entire public record. Roe
  and colleagues examined 2,386 publicly available ECHA compliance-check decisions (*ALTEX*, 2025): over
  70% included at least one adaptation to the standard information requirements, read-across was the most
  common at 48%, and 83 documents contained read-across justifications the agency deemed plausible — "only
  about 7.5% of such adaptation proposals were found to be satisfactory".
- The companion analysis of rejections identified 1,526 rejected read-across hypotheses, of which 70%
  proposed the analogue approach — read-across from a single similar source substance — with analogue
  read-across accounting for roughly 45% of all unsuccessful adaptations. In the testing-proposal stream
  the pattern is the same but milder: of 2,630 proposals from 2008 to 2023, 353 carried adaptations, 304
  of them read-across hypotheses, of which 49% were accepted — 62% for group or category read-across
  against 38% for the analogue approach, group submissions having 2.6 times the odds of acceptance.
- The stake is not paperwork. Read-across is the principal route by which a registrant avoids
  commissioning new animal tests, so the failure of a read-across argument is a decision to test. A 7.5%
  success rate in compliance checks is therefore also a measurement of how often the structural-similarity
  inference is strong enough to carry a hazard conclusion, and the answer the regulator gives, case by
  case, over fifteen years, is: rarely, and least often when the argument is pairwise similarity alone.
- *Claude's gloss:* `non-transitive-at-not-identity` is this result stated as a theorem. Nothing may be
  transported along a reflexive relation that does not compose, and a hazard assessment is exactly the
  sort of thing a transport would have to carry; ECHA's own framework exists to decide, per submission,
  whether the particular inference holds, because no general criterion can be stated — which is the same
  conclusion its substance-identity study reached in the other direction ("no widely applicable sameness
  assessment criteria can be developed", `registrant-practice`). The asymmetry between group and analogue
  approaches is the informative part: a category argument supplies a mechanism, which is a warrant, and
  pairwise similarity supplies a resemblance, which is not. In the construction's terms the acceptable
  cases are those where the registrant exhibited a `Scope` with a reason — a stated property that the
  source and target share and that the hazard depends on — and the rejected ones are those where the
  resemblance was offered as if it were identity. Nine times in ten the resemblance was offered. And the
  cost of the error falls on animals, which is a consequence of a representation defect that nothing in
  the representation records.
