- The form is Part I's, and it holds for every type and every map: for histories `H` and an observation
  `o ∶ H → O`, the fiber `Fibₒ(y) ∶= ∑(h∶H), o(h) = y` (1.1), the recovery `H ≃ ∑(y∶O) Fibₒ(y)` (1.2), and
  `Warrants(y,P) ∶= ∏((h,p)∶Fibₒ(y)) P(h)` (1.3). For a protein, `H` is the chain's trajectories under its
  conditions — the chain as synthesized, the solvent, the other molecules present — and `o` the structure
  an experiment reports. "The native structure" is a reading `y`; the folding question is the shape of the
  fiber over the reading of sequence and conditions. One basin every trajectory ends in is consistent
  folding. A basin that is not the lowest, reached every time, is kinetic control. Two basins with a
  measured rate between them is a fold-switching protein. Each is determined; what varies is the shape, and
  each literature below supplies one shape with its numbers.
