### structure-predictor-carrier
- AlphaFold 2's structure module built in equivariance under rotations and translations, through frames;
  AlphaFold 3 dropped it, replacing the structure module with a diffusion module that predicts raw atom
  coordinates (Abramson et al., *Nature*, 2024). "Categorification of Chemical Reactions: a bottom-up tower
  from stoichiometry to quantum structure" (arXiv:2605.14974, 2026), which classifies architectures by the
  symmetries they hold, records that AF3's denoiser is a standard Transformer whose SE(3) symmetry comes from
  rotation and translation augmentation, with no reflection, so the equivariance is learned in training and
  not architectural, and cites the 4.4% chirality-violation rate on PoseBusters that AF3's own paper reports
  as its certificate.
- The chirality record. AF3 penalises chirality violations in its ranking formula and still reaches 4.4% on
  PoseBusters. On D-peptides ("Has AlphaFold 3 Solved the Protein Folding Problem for D-Peptides?",
  bioRxiv 2025.03.14.643307) the rate is about 50% — 51–52% averaged over seeds for individual complexes —
  although the inputs specified D-chirality at every stereocentre, and more seeds did not improve the
  top-ranked predictions. A restraint-guided inference method fixes it without retraining, applying
  stereochemical restraints at each step of the reverse diffusion and reaching 100% chirality accuracy; it
  targets sp³ carbons through the chiral volume `V = (xᵢ − xⱼ) · ((xᵢ − xₖ) × (xᵢ − xₗ))` over a centre's
  neighbours `j, k, l`. An SO(3)-equivariant diffusion module ("Pearl", arXiv:2510.24670) builds rotation
  back into the architecture and reports higher rates of accurate, physically valid poses than AF3 on
  PoseBusters and Runs N' Poses.
- The fiber. EMBL-EBI's AlphaFold course lists among AF3's limitations that it predicts a single structure
  for a given sequence; for cereblon, which has open and closed states, AF3 predicts the closed state for
  both apo and holo, in all ten overlaid samples.
- *Claude's gloss:* the sign of the chiral volume is unchanged by cyclic permutation of `(j, k, l)` and
  flipped by any transposition, which is the invariant `Cyc` holds as a type: points the triples of
  neighbours, paths the three-cycles, `cyc-sign` defined on the points and carried along the rotations,
  `cyc-swap-differs` and `cyc-mirror-swap-same`. The restraint computes from coordinates, at inference, the
  number whose sign `Cyc` holds by construction. The 4.4% and the 50% are what an invariant held only in the
  training data and a ranking penalty costs: the output type contains wrong-handed molecules, so the model can
  produce them, and a predictor whose output type were `Stereo` could not, because the type has none. That
  is "they don't track identity/invariances losslessly" measured by the predictor's own literature, and it
  is `pose-validity`'s finding one step on — PoseBusters supplied the warrant after the fact; the restraint
  supplies the invariant after the fact. A single structure per sequence is a function from sequence to a
  point, so for lymphotactin, PAI-1, αLP and cereblon the predictor collapses a fiber to one reading and
  keeps no record of the collapse; as a `Report` its warrant is its training set, which `der-retracted`
  makes the dependency through which a flagged structure (`corpora-and-licences`: 992 in the CSD) reaches
  every model trained on it. The accuracy on single-basin proteins is real and is not the question: the
  output type has no place for the family over a reading (1.1), the warrant on what it licenses (1.3), the
  stereochemical invariant, or the window-relative identity of interconverting forms. And `docking-observation`
  already records the other half from the ligand side: protein conformation is not among the degrees of
  freedom docking searches.
