### contact-map-mirror
- Vendruscolo, Kussell and Domany, "Recovery of protein structure from contact maps" (*Folding & Design*
  2:295, 1997; arXiv:cond-mat/9705211): an algorithm that recovers a protein's 3D structure from its contact
  map; for a physically realizable target map the reconstruction has essentially the same map, and the
  reconstructed and original structures agree up to the resolution of the contact-map representation.
- So the contact map — a symmetric relation on residues — is a sufficient combinatorial carrier for the fold
  at its resolution. It is a graph, and the whole combinatorial layer comes before any coordinates; coordinates
  are the last stage, a realization of the graph.
- *Claude's gloss, the fact this section adds:* a contact map is a set of pairs at short distance, so it is
  unchanged by reflecting the structure; every structure and its mirror image have the same contact map, and
  more generally the same matrix of pairwise distances, because distances determine a point set only up to
  rigid motions *including* reflections. A contact map therefore determines a fold only up to its mirror image.
  This is `nmr-orbits`' valine case at the fold scale: valine's bond graph has an automorphism exchanging the
  two methyls that belongs to the graph and not to the configured molecule, and the published spectrum refutes
  it; a protein's contact map is invariant under reflection, which belongs to the map and not to the protein,
  because the chain is built from L-amino acids and its mirror image is another molecule. At both scales the
  graph forgets the arrangement, and identity has to carry it. A reconstruction from contacts alone returns a
  structure or its mirror image and something outside the map has to choose; with handedness carried as data
  (`fold-handedness`), the mirror image is not an inhabitant of the output type — the AlphaFold 3 chirality
  argument of `structure-predictor-carrier`, one scale up.
