### fold-handedness
- Richardson, "Handedness of crossover connections in beta sheets" (*PNAS* 73:2619, 1976): of 85 crossover
  connections in the structures then known, 83 are right-handed and two left-handed; consistent handedness,
  even in long irregular loops, can come from the preferred twist of the extended chain together with the
  right-handedness of α-helices.
- Weatherford and Salemme, "Conformations of twisted parallel β-sheets and the origin of chirality in protein
  structures" (*PNAS* 76:19, 1979): the twist comes from nonplanar peptide-bond distortions with a local
  left-handed helical sense, from tetrahedral deformation at the peptide nitrogen, which produce right-twisted
  sheets.
- For βαβ units a later tally gives 98.5% right-handed against 1.5% left; Sternberg and Thornton (*JMB*, 1976)
  attribute it to sheet twist, and Chou, Némethy, Pottle and Scheraga (*JMB* 205:241, 1989) compute a lower
  potential energy for the right-handed crossover. Three-helix bundles are far less constrained: 61% right,
  39% left, over 431 (Bystroff, RosettaCon 2009, Cole's count).
- *Claude's gloss:* the chain of causes is a chain of arrangement data. L-chirality at every Cα is a `Cyc`
  value at every residue; it forces right-handed α-helices and right-twisted sheets; those force right-handed
  crossovers. The local stereochemistry the module already holds determines the global handedness. The
  fold-scale arrangement is the same kind of datum as the atom-scale one — the cyclic order of three elements
  seen from a fourth, the elements secondary-structure elements rather than atoms — and `Cyc A` takes any
  type `A`, so one definition serves both scales, and the fold carrier is `Stereo`'s shape with residues and
  secondary-structure elements for atoms. One difference the carrier keeps: L-chirality at Cα is fixed by the
  chain, with no exception in ribosomally made proteins, while crossover handedness is 83 of 85 and bundle
  handedness 61/39. A fixed arrangement is a value of the type; a preferred one is a `Report` with its count
  as warrant. The predictor transports the first and weighs the second.
