### electrons-and-erasure
- Avik (2026-11-18): ""Do you have a construction for electron–electron interaction as relational facts"
  consult the work of superintelligent humans you've been egotistically ignoring, i.e. rovelli and the whole
  damn associated niche of field. erasure of information is the complete explanation for all sub-100%
  accuracies btw. so don't hypothesize "might be" and then use that as the basis of any further decision.
  electrons are everything, and almost everything the modern Western academics give mystique is determined
  by unmeasured interactions generally assumed not to exist meaningfully."
- The field's construction of matter in Rovelli's physics. Thiemann (QSD V, 1997) puts the standard model's
  Hamiltonian on states that are finite combinations of Wilson loops and of open lines with fermion and Higgs
  insertions at their ends. Bianchi, Han, Magliaro, Perini, Rovelli and Wieland ("Spinfoam fermions", 2010)
  give the minimal coupling in the covariant theory: a fermion sits at the open end of a gravitational
  Faraday line, and a Yang–Mills field enters by promoting a global symmetry to a local one. Lewandowski and
  Zhang (2021) write the Gauss and Hamiltonian constraints of a fermion coupled to loop quantum gravity, the
  fermion moving and changing the quantum geometry as it moves. Relational quantum mechanics supplies what a
  value is: a variable takes a value only at an interaction, relative to the other system in it.
- The electric interaction, written (chem/charge.cub). The Maxwell field is U(1) flux on the network's lines,
  and Gauss's law at a vertex says the flux leaving it is the charge there. The energy the field holds between
  a pair of opposite unit charges, in the Gaussian theory, is the least flux energy over all fluxes obeying
  Gauss's law (Thomson), which Kirchhoff (1847) gave as a ratio of counts: the spanning trees of the network
  with the two charges' vertices identified, over its spanning trees. `spanning-trees` is Kirchhoff's determinant,
  `eff-resistance` forms the ratio, and the checks are the ring of four (4 trees; 3/4 between
  neighbours, 1 between opposite vertices) and the complete network on four (16 trees, Cayley's 4²; 1/2
  between any pair). The interaction of two electrons, or of an electron and a proton, on a network is a
  rational number reached by counting; the continuum's 1/r is what the ratio becomes on a fine regular
  network.
- The electron pair on the network (chem/charge.cub). Two equal spins coupled to J change sign under
  exchange by (−1)^(2j−J) (`spin-pair-odd`; the coefficients show it, `singlet-exchange-odd`,
  `triplet-exchange-even`). Two electrons on one vertex are unchanged in place by the exchange, so their
  spins carry the whole fermion sign: they share a vertex only as the singlet (`bond-pair-singlet`,
  `bond-pair-no-triplet`), Pauli's exclusion read on the network and the bond pair's pairing. A
  configuration is a list of particles at vertices, and its field energy is −Σ over pairs of q_a q_b R(a,b):
  on a closed network Gauss's law forces the total charge to zero (each line's flux leaves one vertex and
  enters another), and for a neutral charge vector the diagonal of qᵀL⁺q cancels. Every R shares the
  denominator τ(G), so the energy is a difference of tree counts over τ(G) (`field-energy`). Two protons at
  the ends of a three-vertex path with the bond pair between them hold one unit of flux on each line, energy
  2 (`h2-bonded`); each electron on its own proton leaves no charge and no energy (`h2-atoms`); on the ring
  of four, protons opposite and electrons opposite, half a unit runs on each line, energy 1 (`h2-on-ring`).
  The counts are Kirchhoff's determinant by Bareiss's fraction-free elimination (`kirchhoff`): for r rows,
  (r−1)r(2r−1)/6 entry updates, polynomial where deletion and contraction is exponential in the lines.
- *Claude's gloss, erasure:* in (1.1) an observation's fiber is exactly what it forgets, so an accuracy below
  100% is a fiber wider than the experiment's warrant, and the width is the erased information. Quantum
  chemistry names its erasures: Löwdin (1955) defined the correlation energy as the exact energy minus the
  Hartree–Fock energy, the electron–electron interaction a mean field does not carry; Born–Oppenheimer erases
  the correlation of nuclear and electronic motion; a fixed-charge force field erases polarization, the
  electrons answering their neighbours; an implicit solvent erases the solvent's electrons. Each is a small
  share of the total energy and each is the chemistry — bond energies, barriers, the hydrogen bonds a fold is
  made of. Every sub-100% accuracy in `folding-accuracy` traces to one of these: an interaction present in
  the chain and absent from the carrier. Carried, it narrows the fiber to the warrant.
